# Copy to .env next to docker-compose.yml. Compose reads it automatically.

# The host folder Agent Mode may edit. It is mounted at /workspace inside the
# container, so a project at ~/code/my-app is opened as /workspace/code/my-app.
#
# Defaults to your home directory, which is why projects can be opened where they
# already live rather than being moved into a staging folder. Leave it unset to
# accept that, or set it to narrow what the agent can reach:
#
#   macOS         /Users/you
#   Linux / WSL2  /home/you        (keep projects on the Linux side of WSL2: a
#                                   /mnt/c path crosses a bridge and is slow)
#   Windows       C:\Users\you
#
# Mounting your home directory does not make all of it a project. /workspace
# itself is browse-only, and the broad folders (Desktop, Documents, Downloads,
# Library, AppData, ...) are refused as destinations -- you browse through them to
# the project inside. Hidden folders are ordinary: ~/.dotfiles opens like anything
# else. What the agent may do once a folder is open is decided per-operation by
# the permission resolver, not by this mount.
# NEO_WORKSPACE_HOST_ROOT=/Users/you

# Linux only. A bind mount keeps the host's ownership, so the container has to
# run as you or every write to your folder fails with "Permission denied".
# Run `id -u` and put the number here. Leave NEO_GID alone: /app/data is
# group-owned by 0 and group-writable so that any uid can still use it.
# Unnecessary on macOS and Windows, where Docker Desktop maps ownership for you.
# NEO_UID=1000
